1
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,160
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:38,303
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:51,943
Whew. Oh.

5
00:01:52,012 --> 00:01:53,578
Good morning.

6
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:07,158
What's going on?

7
00:02:07,227 --> 00:02:08,660
Well, finally.

8
00:02:08,729 --> 00:02:10,562
I've been waiting since 8:30.

9
00:02:10,631 --> 00:02:12,331
Where's Mr. Matlock?

10
00:02:12,399 --> 00:02:15,733
Um... who are you?

11
00:02:15,803 --> 00:02:18,336
I'm Ned Collins with the<i>
Atlanta Digest</i> magazine.

12
00:02:18,405 --> 00:02:20,338
Mr. Matlock didn't tell
you about our interview?

13
00:02:22,409 --> 00:02:24,009
No, he never mentioned it.

14
00:02:25,679 --> 00:02:27,912
That's strange.

15
00:02:27,981 --> 00:02:30,548
An interview with<i> Atlanta
Digest</i> is the kind of thing

16
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:34,852
Ben would grumble
about but secretly enjoy.

17
00:02:37,290 --> 00:02:39,524
How did you get in here?

18
00:02:39,593 --> 00:02:41,626
Security guard.

19
00:02:41,695 --> 00:02:44,630
Nice guy. Let me stand out in
the rain for a good 20 minutes.

20
00:02:44,698 --> 00:02:45,698
Oh.

21
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,834
Don't.

22
00:02:48,903 --> 00:02:51,136
Are you all right, Mr. Collins?

23
00:02:51,204 --> 00:02:52,537
Yeah, fine.

24
00:02:52,606 --> 00:02:54,117
I, uh, I guess it's the rain.

25
00:02:54,141 --> 00:02:55,551
It gets on my nerves. Yeah.

26
00:02:55,575 --> 00:02:57,186
I didn't mean to
bark at you like that.

27
00:02:57,210 --> 00:02:58,309
I understand.

28
00:02:58,378 --> 00:02:59,823
I guess it's one of those days.

29
00:02:59,847 --> 00:03:01,847
Yeah, it is.

30
00:03:01,915 --> 00:03:03,248
Well, I need coffee.

31
00:03:03,317 --> 00:03:04,416
You want some?

32
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:05,984
Yeah, about a quart.

33
00:03:08,055 --> 00:03:10,455
So you expect
Mr. Matlock any minute?

34
00:03:10,523 --> 00:03:13,792
Well, ordinarily,
Ben's the first one in.

35
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:15,928
He gets up at 5:00.

36
00:03:15,996 --> 00:03:18,930
He says early morning's
his thinking time.

37
00:03:26,072 --> 00:03:28,150
I don't even know my
own name at that hour.

38
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:30,453
But this morning,

39
00:03:30,477 --> 00:03:33,037
Ben had to stop off
at the courthouse.

40
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:37,583
Courthouse?

41
00:03:37,651 --> 00:03:39,918
When will he be here?

42
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:41,819
Not long.

43
00:03:41,888 --> 00:03:43,733
And he's worth
waiting for, Mr. Collins.

44
00:03:43,757 --> 00:03:45,435
Ben is the best storyteller.

45
00:03:45,459 --> 00:03:48,026
Yeah, so I've heard.

46
00:03:48,094 --> 00:03:50,294
Of course, he's been
known to embellish

47
00:03:50,363 --> 00:03:52,430
just a little.

48
00:03:52,499 --> 00:03:54,399
I've heard that, too.

49
00:04:01,308 --> 00:04:03,174
You can't do that.

50
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:05,543
Like hell, I can't.

51
00:04:05,612 --> 00:04:08,080
Ben doesn't allow anyone
to smoke in this office,

52
00:04:08,148 --> 00:04:10,615
not even clients who
are paying him a fortune.

53
00:04:10,684 --> 00:04:12,717
Now, if you light that,

54
00:04:12,786 --> 00:04:14,946
you'll be out on your
ear without an interview.

55
00:04:14,988 --> 00:04:16,554
I wouldn't want that.

56
00:04:23,596 --> 00:04:24,929
Oh, that startled me.

57
00:04:26,166 --> 00:04:28,066
That was so loud, huh?

58
00:04:29,336 --> 00:04:32,337
I like rain, but
thunder, I don't know.

59
00:04:32,406 --> 00:04:35,240
Oh, boy, this is a mess.

60
00:04:35,309 --> 00:04:37,354
Like I said, it's just
one of those days.

61
00:04:37,378 --> 00:04:39,744
Yeah.

62
00:04:39,813 --> 00:04:41,146
Got to get some carpet cleaner.

63
00:04:41,215 --> 00:04:42,580
Where you going?

64
00:04:42,649 --> 00:04:44,883
The janitorial closet has
some cleaning supplies.

65
00:04:44,951 --> 00:04:46,095
The security guard has the key.

66
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:47,799
Wait.

67
00:04:50,557 --> 00:04:52,457
Oh, the power's going out.

68
00:04:52,526 --> 00:04:54,092
Yeah, it seems that way.

69
00:04:54,161 --> 00:04:56,228
Look, I have an idea.

70
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:57,862
You work side by
side with Matlock.

71
00:04:57,931 --> 00:04:59,731
Who knows him better?

72
00:04:59,799 --> 00:05:02,100
Till he arrives, why
don't I just interview you?

73
00:05:02,169 --> 00:05:03,735
No. No, no, no.

74
00:05:03,804 --> 00:05:05,281
Well, he can hardly
sit here himself

75
00:05:05,305 --> 00:05:07,171
and talk about how great he is.

76
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:10,275
But he does, all the time.

77
00:05:10,344 --> 00:05:12,277
And he is.

78
00:05:14,581 --> 00:05:17,315
Well, Ben's not only the best
lawyer I've ever worked with

79
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:18,984
or the best lawyer
in Atlanta... Mm-hmm.

80
00:05:19,052 --> 00:05:22,653
But the best, period.

81
00:05:22,722 --> 00:05:24,956
To, uh, you know,
to watch him work,

82
00:05:25,025 --> 00:05:28,460
put all the pieces
together, he's amazing.

83
00:05:28,529 --> 00:05:31,129
And in the courtroom,
no one is better.

84
00:05:32,999 --> 00:05:34,833
I remember this one case

85
00:05:34,902 --> 00:05:37,502
when even I wasn't
prepared for what he did.

86
00:05:37,571 --> 00:05:40,605
Members of the jury...

87
00:05:40,674 --> 00:05:43,808
I know we'd all like to get
out of here and go someplace

88
00:05:43,877 --> 00:05:45,788
where the air
conditioning actually works,

89
00:05:45,812 --> 00:05:48,280
so I'm gonna be brief.

90
00:05:48,348 --> 00:05:51,850
Now, I've got to be
fair and recognize,

91
00:05:51,918 --> 00:05:55,319
as do you, that the prosecution

92
00:05:55,388 --> 00:05:57,756
has collected a lot

93
00:05:57,824 --> 00:06:00,525
of circumstantial evidence.

94
00:06:00,593 --> 00:06:02,961
But the problem is,

95
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:05,897
it is all circumstantial.

96
00:06:05,966 --> 00:06:09,067
Now, they've accused
this man of murder.

97
00:06:09,135 --> 00:06:12,003
But no one saw the crime occur.

98
00:06:12,072 --> 00:06:14,773
No body has ever been found.

99
00:06:14,841 --> 00:06:18,176
No one knows how the
victim died or if he died at all.

100
00:06:18,244 --> 00:06:21,345
In fact, they have never proved

101
00:06:21,415 --> 00:06:25,016
a crime was actually committed.

102
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:31,523
And now... I'm
gonna count to ten.

103
00:06:31,591 --> 00:06:34,326
And when I'm done,

104
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:36,628
this supposed
victim's gonna walk

105
00:06:36,697 --> 00:06:38,162
through that door over there.

106
00:06:41,935 --> 00:06:49,935
One... two...
three... four... five...

107
00:06:53,079 --> 00:07:01,079
six... seven...
eight... nine... ten.

108
00:07:14,935 --> 00:07:18,603
So you see, members of the jury,

109
00:07:18,672 --> 00:07:21,272
in fairness,

110
00:07:21,341 --> 00:07:24,509
you must return a
verdict of not guilty.

111
00:07:24,577 --> 00:07:26,744
Each and every one of you

112
00:07:26,813 --> 00:07:28,913
was staring at that door,

113
00:07:28,982 --> 00:07:31,783
because you had
a reasonable doubt

114
00:07:31,852 --> 00:07:35,153
that a crime was even committed.

115
00:07:35,221 --> 00:07:38,056
And you must let
this man go free.

116
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:41,059
He was staring at that
door as hard as you were.

117
00:07:43,129 --> 00:07:44,129
And so were they.

118
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:52,370
Dramatic, huh?

119
00:07:52,438 --> 00:07:53,637
Very.

120
00:07:53,706 --> 00:07:55,146
Sounds like he'll
do anything to win.

121
00:07:55,208 --> 00:07:56,640
Mm-hmm.

122
00:07:56,709 --> 00:07:59,155
Ben knows just how
to appeal to a jury.

123
00:07:59,179 --> 00:08:02,280
He's a brilliant lawyer.

124
00:08:02,349 --> 00:08:03,948
And teacher.

125
00:08:05,385 --> 00:08:06,917
And friend.

126
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:10,388
He's a good friend.

127
00:08:12,259 --> 00:08:15,192
One time, my best
friend from college

128
00:08:15,262 --> 00:08:18,363
Andrea Morrow, was
accused of murder,

129
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:20,498
and it turned
into the strangest,

130
00:08:20,567 --> 00:08:22,333
most unique case.

131
00:08:36,950 --> 00:08:39,751
♪ ♪

132
00:08:55,602 --> 00:08:57,162
Bang.

133
00:08:59,372 --> 00:09:03,508
The killer was blind
and very, very clever.

134
00:09:48,621 --> 00:09:50,066
Ah.

135
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:52,223
The electricity's gone out.

136
00:09:52,292 --> 00:09:53,425
I wouldn't know.

137
00:09:53,493 --> 00:09:55,160
It's-it's a circuit breaker.

138
00:09:55,228 --> 00:09:56,527
What are you doing here, anyway?

139
00:09:56,596 --> 00:09:58,274
Just taking care of
some old business.

140
00:09:58,298 --> 00:09:59,730
All alone in the dark.

141
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:18,149
You should be ashamed
for even thinking such a thing.

142
00:10:19,819 --> 00:10:22,787
Clay Graham was my best friend.

143
00:10:22,856 --> 00:10:25,689
I wouldn't have killed
him even if I could.

144
00:10:29,029 --> 00:10:33,364
Quite obviously... I can't.

145
00:10:37,403 --> 00:10:40,705
I understand that you
were quite a marksman

146
00:10:40,773 --> 00:10:42,506
before you lost your sight.

147
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:49,013
Mr. Matlock, I ask
you, truthfully...

148
00:10:52,519 --> 00:10:54,439
are these the eyes of a killer?

149
00:10:59,826 --> 00:11:02,371
<i>Well, the evidence
against Andrea</i>

150
00:11:02,395 --> 00:11:04,796
<i>was compelling,
and as I'd feared,</i>

151
00:11:04,865 --> 00:11:06,865
<i>the prosecution
put me on the stand.</i>

152
00:11:06,899 --> 00:11:08,399
Sure, I did.

153
00:11:08,468 --> 00:11:11,136
<i>Well, I was afraid my testimony
would convict my friend.</i>

154
00:11:11,204 --> 00:11:13,437
The defendant say, Miss Thomas?

155
00:11:13,506 --> 00:11:15,607
I don't recall her exact words.

156
00:11:15,675 --> 00:11:16,675
Well, then would you

157
00:11:16,710 --> 00:11:18,443
paraphrase it for us, please?

158
00:11:21,014 --> 00:11:24,015
She said, "No."

159
00:11:24,084 --> 00:11:25,694
Um, he'd hit her
for the last time,

160
00:11:25,718 --> 00:11:27,563
and she wasn't gonna
let him do it again,

161
00:11:27,587 --> 00:11:30,355
no matter what it takes.

162
00:11:30,423 --> 00:11:31,356
Thank you.

163
00:11:31,424 --> 00:11:34,025
No further questions,
Your Honor.

164
00:11:36,162 --> 00:11:37,896
Cross, Mr. Matlock?

165
00:11:37,964 --> 00:11:41,165
I'd like to examine the phrase

166
00:11:41,233 --> 00:11:43,000
"No matter what it takes."

167
00:11:43,069 --> 00:11:44,702
Seems to me, that's a phrase

168
00:11:44,770 --> 00:11:46,871
that most of us use
almost every day.

169
00:11:46,939 --> 00:11:49,273
"I'll do such and such,
no matter what it takes."

170
00:11:49,342 --> 00:11:52,410
When I was 11 or 12 years old,

171
00:11:52,479 --> 00:11:54,778
my mother said, "Next
Sunday, you're gonna learn

172
00:11:54,847 --> 00:11:56,692
to make cornbread,
no matter what it takes."

173
00:11:56,716 --> 00:11:58,527
And I don't think
she meant to kill me.

174
00:11:58,551 --> 00:11:59,484
Objection, Your Honor.

175
00:11:59,552 --> 00:12:00,952
Nothing further.

176
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:02,820
Objection, Your Honor.

177
00:12:04,891 --> 00:12:08,493
But Ben took what I'd
said and proved otherwise.

178
00:12:08,561 --> 00:12:12,230
Another Ben Matlock con.

179
00:12:12,298 --> 00:12:13,798
Not really.

180
00:12:13,867 --> 00:12:15,711
More like another point of view.

181
00:12:15,735 --> 00:12:16,801
Hmm...

182
00:12:16,870 --> 00:12:19,348
And then in the course
of my investigation,

183
00:12:19,372 --> 00:12:22,106
<i>I uncovered an important clue.</i>

184
00:12:38,225 --> 00:12:40,458
<i>And then he went
back to his studio</i>

185
00:12:40,526 --> 00:12:41,993
and did some target practice.

186
00:12:42,062 --> 00:12:43,572
<i>And then, later, at
the victim's house,</i>

187
00:12:43,596 --> 00:12:47,165
<i>Ben uncovered the one clue
that everyone had overlooked.</i>

188
00:12:47,234 --> 00:12:50,201
Yeah, yeah. There's something...

189
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:53,071
Something doesn't quite follow.

190
00:12:53,140 --> 00:12:54,338
What?

191
00:12:54,407 --> 00:12:56,685
Well, the forensics
people estimated,

192
00:12:56,709 --> 00:12:58,821
from the trajectory
of the bullet,

193
00:12:58,845 --> 00:13:03,381
that the victim was standing on
this third step when he was hit.

194
00:13:03,449 --> 00:13:04,949
Right.

195
00:13:05,018 --> 00:13:08,486
But look at this
little trail of blood

196
00:13:08,555 --> 00:13:10,922
from the cut on his right foot.

197
00:13:12,926 --> 00:13:15,193
There's blood
here on the landing

198
00:13:15,261 --> 00:13:18,696
and on this first step
and then on the second,

199
00:13:18,765 --> 00:13:20,810
and if their
estimate is correct,

200
00:13:20,834 --> 00:13:23,434
there should be blood
on this step, but there isn't.

201
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:28,706
Oh, I'm sorry.

202
00:13:28,775 --> 00:13:29,975
We didn't mean to startle you.

203
00:13:30,043 --> 00:13:32,443
I'm Ben Matlock. This
is Michelle Thomas.

204
00:13:32,512 --> 00:13:34,579
We represent Andrea Morrow.

205
00:13:34,647 --> 00:13:37,248
Hattie Larson, uh,
Mr. Graham's housekeeper.

206
00:13:37,317 --> 00:13:40,351
I-I came here to clear out
my things and to leave the key.

207
00:13:44,223 --> 00:13:46,724
Ms. Larson...

208
00:13:46,793 --> 00:13:50,294
Uh, how often did you come
in and clean for Mr. Graham?

209
00:13:50,363 --> 00:13:53,464
I came in at 10:00, left at
5:00, day in and day out.

210
00:13:53,533 --> 00:13:56,734
Hmm. And how often did
you empty the wastebasket?

211
00:13:56,803 --> 00:13:57,935
Every day.

212
00:13:58,004 --> 00:14:00,504
So, the last time
this one was emptied

213
00:14:00,573 --> 00:14:01,973
was the day he was murdered?

214
00:14:02,041 --> 00:14:03,041
That's right.

215
00:14:04,677 --> 00:14:09,347
Mr. Matlock, I was
nowhere near that place.

216
00:14:09,415 --> 00:14:11,515
I was at my
apartment that night.

217
00:14:11,584 --> 00:14:13,284
All night.

218
00:14:17,690 --> 00:14:20,825
Your Honor, at this time...

219
00:14:20,893 --> 00:14:25,196
I'd like to enter into
evidence defense exhibit "G,"

220
00:14:25,265 --> 00:14:27,231
this piece of paper.

221
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:31,301
It was found in a wastebasket

222
00:14:31,370 --> 00:14:34,304
in the kitchen of
Clay Graham's home.

223
00:14:38,210 --> 00:14:40,756
Now, Mr. Graham's
housekeeper is certain

224
00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:44,015
that wastebasket was empty

225
00:14:44,083 --> 00:14:49,153
when she left the house at
5:00 the day of the murder,

226
00:14:49,221 --> 00:14:52,289
which means this piece of paper

227
00:14:52,358 --> 00:14:55,993
had to be thrown
there by the killer.

228
00:14:58,231 --> 00:14:59,731
Do you know what's on it?

229
00:14:59,799 --> 00:15:01,933
I have no idea.

230
00:15:02,002 --> 00:15:06,471
Clay Graham stepping
on this piece of paper

231
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:11,142
was your cue when
and where to fire.

232
00:15:11,211 --> 00:15:14,345
You had placed this

233
00:15:14,414 --> 00:15:17,248
<i>on the third step from the top.</i>

234
00:15:17,317 --> 00:15:21,685
And when you heard
Clay Graham's foot

235
00:15:21,755 --> 00:15:24,488
<i>hit this paper, you shot him.</i>

236
00:15:28,828 --> 00:15:32,797
When you retrieved the piece
of paper and threw it away,

237
00:15:32,865 --> 00:15:37,901
how could you know it had
Clay Graham's blood on it?

238
00:15:37,970 --> 00:15:41,238
You had planned
everything so carefully.

239
00:15:41,308 --> 00:15:46,210
I guess you thought this was
a blank piece of drawing paper.

240
00:15:49,816 --> 00:15:51,316
Nothing further.

241
00:15:57,924 --> 00:16:00,958
So, Matlock got your friend off.

242
00:16:01,027 --> 00:16:02,894
She was innocent.

243
00:16:04,764 --> 00:16:06,044
Well, maybe that's Ben.

244
00:16:06,099 --> 00:16:08,165
I'll tell him you're waiting.

245
00:16:11,671 --> 00:16:13,070
Hello?

246
00:16:13,139 --> 00:16:14,539
Ben.

247
00:16:14,607 --> 00:16:16,874
Yeah, I got soaked, too.

248
00:16:16,943 --> 00:16:18,209
Ben?

249
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:20,845
Yeah, cats and dogs.

250
00:16:20,913 --> 00:16:22,714
Ben...

251
00:16:22,782 --> 00:16:26,050
They're predicting another
inch before nightfall?

252
00:16:26,118 --> 00:16:29,721
Uh, more lightning
and thunder, too?

253
00:16:29,789 --> 00:16:31,556
You're kidding.

254
00:16:31,624 --> 00:16:32,957
Ben...

255
00:16:34,828 --> 00:16:36,794
Do as I say and
I won't hurt you.

256
00:16:36,863 --> 00:16:39,664
Don't scream, understand?

257
00:16:39,733 --> 00:16:42,166
Tell him to come to the
office right now, got it?

258
00:16:42,235 --> 00:16:43,300
Now.

259
00:16:46,573 --> 00:16:48,539
Ben?

260
00:16:48,608 --> 00:16:50,753
Yes, that
intersection is flooded.

261
00:16:50,777 --> 00:16:52,343
Now! Ben, if you could

262
00:16:52,412 --> 00:16:54,345
come to the office...

263
00:16:54,414 --> 00:16:56,347
Okay. No, that's fine.

264
00:16:56,416 --> 00:16:58,215
Good-bye.

265
00:17:00,119 --> 00:17:01,119
Well?

266
00:17:01,153 --> 00:17:02,964
He'll be here as soon as he can.

267
00:17:06,159 --> 00:17:07,825
That was the best I could do.

268
00:17:07,894 --> 00:17:09,360
No, it wasn't.

269
00:17:20,573 --> 00:17:23,118
All right, Miss Thomas, I
want you to pick up the phone,

270
00:17:23,142 --> 00:17:25,710
call Matlock and tell
him to come here now.

271
00:17:25,778 --> 00:17:27,878
But I don't know
where to call him.

272
00:17:27,947 --> 00:17:30,080
Don't lie to me.

273
00:17:30,149 --> 00:17:32,627
I have been lied to
and lied to. No more.

274
00:17:32,651 --> 00:17:34,218
I'm not lying to
you, Mr. Collins.

275
00:17:34,287 --> 00:17:37,188
When Ben called, he
didn't say where he was.

276
00:17:37,257 --> 00:17:38,537
You said the courthouse.

277
00:17:38,591 --> 00:17:41,059
Well, yes, but the
courthouse is a big place.

278
00:17:41,127 --> 00:17:43,239
He could have called
from almost any office

279
00:17:43,263 --> 00:17:47,464
or from a phone
booth in a corridor or...

280
00:17:47,534 --> 00:17:49,667
I honestly don't know
where to reach him.

281
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:50,835
I honestly don't.

282
00:17:50,904 --> 00:17:53,070
You're making me
angry, Miss Thomas.

283
00:17:56,575 --> 00:17:58,242
Ooh!

284
00:17:58,311 --> 00:17:59,343
Oh!

285
00:17:59,412 --> 00:18:01,624
One word, one
sound, you're dead.

286
00:18:01,648 --> 00:18:02,747
That wind!

287
00:18:02,815 --> 00:18:05,082
Oh, it's like a
hurricane out there.

288
00:18:05,151 --> 00:18:06,550
Hi, Michelle.

289
00:18:06,619 --> 00:18:09,286
Isn't this weather awful?

290
00:18:09,355 --> 00:18:12,223
Is someone having
their picture taken?

291
00:18:12,292 --> 00:18:15,859
Ben Matlock for the, uh,
the cover of<i> Atlanta Digest.</i>

292
00:18:15,929 --> 00:18:19,062
The cover? Oh,
well, I'm impressed.

293
00:18:19,131 --> 00:18:21,499
That rascal didn't tell me.

294
00:18:21,567 --> 00:18:23,545
Uh, Mr. Matlock's at the
courthouse this morning.

295
00:18:23,569 --> 00:18:24,713
You can find him there.

296
00:18:24,737 --> 00:18:26,070
I just saw him there.

297
00:18:26,139 --> 00:18:27,682
We're going to
meet here to go over

298
00:18:27,706 --> 00:18:29,640
our plans for the Bar
Association meeting.

299
00:18:29,709 --> 00:18:33,777
Last year, we
raised nearly 20...

300
00:18:33,846 --> 00:18:38,549
Uh... is something wrong?

301
00:18:39,652 --> 00:18:40,918
I'll...

302
00:18:42,622 --> 00:18:46,056
I... I'll just go find
Ben at the courthouse.

303
00:18:46,125 --> 00:18:48,325
Don't worry. I'll
see myself out.

304
00:18:48,394 --> 00:18:49,527
I don't think so!

305
00:18:49,595 --> 00:18:51,529
Oh!

306
00:18:51,598 --> 00:18:54,158
Don't scream. Understand?

307
00:18:56,602 --> 00:18:58,169
Sit.

308
00:18:59,338 --> 00:19:01,272
Sit!

309
00:19:03,376 --> 00:19:05,936
Over here, with your friend.

310
00:19:06,679 --> 00:19:08,245
Come on!

311
00:19:12,685 --> 00:19:14,118
Not too close.

312
00:19:17,557 --> 00:19:19,590
You two are smart.

313
00:19:19,659 --> 00:19:21,992
Too smart.

314
00:19:22,061 --> 00:19:24,895
Now we'll all wait for
Ben Matlock together.

315
00:19:27,767 --> 00:19:29,200
We haven't met.

316
00:19:29,268 --> 00:19:31,335
I'm Julie March and you're...?

317
00:19:31,404 --> 00:19:34,104
The man who is going
to kill Ben Matlock.

318
00:19:35,174 --> 00:19:37,386
You sound very angry at Ben.

319
00:19:37,410 --> 00:19:39,877
Angry doesn't half cover it.

320
00:19:39,946 --> 00:19:41,891
Maybe if you talked about it.

321
00:19:41,915 --> 00:19:46,384
Uh... Do you know Ben?

322
00:19:46,452 --> 00:19:48,585
No, I never had the pleasure.

323
00:19:48,654 --> 00:19:51,722
Ben Matlock is the finest
man I've ever known.

324
00:19:55,294 --> 00:19:57,454
Look, lady, I've got no
bone to pick with you.

325
00:19:58,531 --> 00:19:59,808
What are you, his girlfriend?

326
00:19:59,832 --> 00:20:02,366
No, not exac... I
wouldn't exactly say that.

327
00:20:02,435 --> 00:20:05,136
Well, what are you, then?

328
00:20:05,205 --> 00:20:08,072
Well, actually,

329
00:20:08,141 --> 00:20:11,676
our relationship is
very complicated.

330
00:20:11,744 --> 00:20:16,947
<i>Right from the beginning,
we had this marvelous rapport</i>

331
00:20:17,016 --> 00:20:21,151
<i>and absolute
respect for each other.</i>

332
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:23,187
You don't think for one second,

333
00:20:23,255 --> 00:20:25,789
I'm going to reveal
my strategy, do you?

334
00:20:25,858 --> 00:20:27,124
Give me a break.

335
00:20:27,193 --> 00:20:29,026
You know the D.A.'s office.

336
00:20:29,095 --> 00:20:30,427
I'm up against the toughest,

337
00:20:30,496 --> 00:20:34,031
wildest, most ruthless
prosecutor they've got.

338
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,866
Thank you, Your Honor.

339
00:20:35,935 --> 00:20:38,502
Now, Mr. Frankel,
you have testified

340
00:20:38,571 --> 00:20:40,771
the trajectory of the
bullet into the chair...

341
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:42,139
Objection!

342
00:20:43,843 --> 00:20:46,210
The chair isn't on trial here.

343
00:20:46,279 --> 00:20:48,279
Let me ask that question again.

344
00:20:48,348 --> 00:20:50,847
Did the chair... Objection.

345
00:20:50,916 --> 00:20:53,094
The chair isn't in the witness
stand, though I'm sure if it

346
00:20:53,118 --> 00:20:54,785
were, Mr. Matlock

347
00:20:54,854 --> 00:20:58,188
would be piercing
in his examination.

348
00:20:58,258 --> 00:21:00,469
Rephrase, Mr. Matlock.

349
00:21:00,493 --> 00:21:01,992
I'd be pleased, Your Honor,

350
00:21:02,061 --> 00:21:04,194
if I could get a gnat's
eye's worth of space

351
00:21:04,264 --> 00:21:05,663
to get my question out.

352
00:21:05,731 --> 00:21:08,176
What the hell
was that all about?

353
00:21:08,200 --> 00:21:09,800
I beg your pardon?

354
00:21:09,869 --> 00:21:11,847
Don't give me
that innocent look.

355
00:21:11,871 --> 00:21:14,431
Of all the unreasonable,
meddlesome objections

356
00:21:14,473 --> 00:21:15,972
I ever heard of.

357
00:21:16,041 --> 00:21:17,674
Ooh, high praise.

358
00:21:17,743 --> 00:21:19,076
And before lunch?

359
00:21:19,145 --> 00:21:20,388
Chair on the witness stand.

360
00:21:20,412 --> 00:21:21,957
I suppose you
thought that was cute?

361
00:21:21,981 --> 00:21:23,313
Oh, come on, Ben.

362
00:21:23,382 --> 00:21:24,848
The jury was falling asleep.

363
00:21:24,917 --> 00:21:26,016
I did you a favor.

364
00:21:26,085 --> 00:21:29,186
Besides, your
client is so guilty,

365
00:21:29,254 --> 00:21:31,521
the only suspense is
when you're going to try

366
00:21:31,591 --> 00:21:32,856
to plea bargain him.

367
00:21:32,925 --> 00:21:35,359
Of course, our
relationship is not

368
00:21:35,428 --> 00:21:36,927
all special moments.

369
00:21:36,996 --> 00:21:40,630
But we are always
the best of friends.

370
00:21:40,699 --> 00:21:42,311
I am merely stating matters
of fact... Your Honor, if you...

371
00:21:42,335 --> 00:21:44,079
If I could... If I could just...
Matters of fact, Your Honor.

372
00:21:44,103 --> 00:21:46,381
Counselors! This
young man's lifestyle...

373
00:21:46,405 --> 00:21:48,005
Are you lecturing me?

374
00:21:48,074 --> 00:21:50,207
No. Counselors!

375
00:21:50,276 --> 00:21:53,610
Will counsel approach the bench?

376
00:21:53,679 --> 00:21:54,923
<i>It isn't always easy.</i>

377
00:21:54,947 --> 00:21:57,747
<i>I mean, I prosecute
the people Ben defends.</i>

378
00:21:57,816 --> 00:22:00,150
<i>So, at times,</i>

379
00:22:00,218 --> 00:22:02,786
<i>we're what you might
call friendly adversaries.</i>

380
00:22:02,855 --> 00:22:04,833
Your Honor, we're just
having a reasoned argument.

381
00:22:04,857 --> 00:22:07,324
Mr. Matlock, that'll be enough.

382
00:22:07,393 --> 00:22:09,559
Back on the record.

383
00:22:09,628 --> 00:22:11,195
As far as bail is concerned,

384
00:22:11,264 --> 00:22:13,463
prosecution's
argument is well-taken.

385
00:22:13,532 --> 00:22:14,631
Bail is denied.

386
00:22:14,700 --> 00:22:16,177
Thank you, Your
Honor. But, Your Honor!

387
00:22:16,201 --> 00:22:17,935
Mr. Matlock, that is all.

388
00:22:20,573 --> 00:22:23,173
I'm sorry, but I do
think that was fair.

389
00:22:23,242 --> 00:22:24,474
Well, I suppose.

390
00:22:24,544 --> 00:22:25,787
Are you coming home for dinner?

391
00:22:25,811 --> 00:22:27,210
Yeah. Good.

392
00:22:27,279 --> 00:22:29,625
I'll make you the best fried
chicken you've ever had.

393
00:22:29,649 --> 00:22:31,169
Yeah? Yeah.

394
00:22:32,151 --> 00:22:33,484
Ben is the most brilliant

395
00:22:33,552 --> 00:22:35,652
legal mind I've
ever encountered.

396
00:22:35,721 --> 00:22:36,920
He's a fraud and a liar.

397
00:22:36,989 --> 00:22:38,132
No, he isn't.

398
00:22:38,156 --> 00:22:39,768
I'm sorry, but you're wrong.

399
00:22:39,792 --> 00:22:40,891
Julie.

400
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:42,258
It's all right, Michelle.

401
00:22:42,327 --> 00:22:45,228
Ben Matlock is not a
fraud, and he is certainly not

402
00:22:45,297 --> 00:22:48,799
a liar. In fact, there
is only one time

403
00:22:48,867 --> 00:22:51,568
when his integrity has
ever been questioned.

404
00:22:51,637 --> 00:22:54,471
And Mr. Matlock said that
if I would vote not guilty,

405
00:22:54,540 --> 00:22:56,840
and get some of the
other jurors to vote with me,

406
00:22:56,909 --> 00:22:59,743
well, um, he'd make
it worth my while.

407
00:22:59,812 --> 00:23:01,144
Worth your while?

408
00:23:01,213 --> 00:23:02,546
$50,000.

409
00:23:02,615 --> 00:23:04,048
50,000?

410
00:23:04,116 --> 00:23:06,850
I... well, I said yes.

411
00:23:06,919 --> 00:23:09,085
And-And as soon as
I said it, I felt terrible.

412
00:23:09,154 --> 00:23:11,466
I wanted to back out, but
Mr. Matlock wouldn't let me.

413
00:23:11,490 --> 00:23:13,490
Your Honor, I
object to all of this.

414
00:23:13,559 --> 00:23:15,192
It's scurrilous and completely

415
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:16,493
without foundation.

416
00:23:16,562 --> 00:23:17,695
Mr. Craven has no

417
00:23:17,763 --> 00:23:19,141
corroboration of any kind.

418
00:23:19,165 --> 00:23:20,441
Let him produce

419
00:23:20,465 --> 00:23:23,267
one piece of evidence to
back up his story... just one!

420
00:23:23,335 --> 00:23:25,402
Mr. Craven?

421
00:23:31,710 --> 00:23:33,444
Where'd you get that envelope?

422
00:23:33,513 --> 00:23:38,615
<i>I knew Ben Matlock would
never do such a thing,</i>

423
00:23:38,684 --> 00:23:40,384
<i>but there was evidence.</i>

424
00:23:40,453 --> 00:23:42,218
<i>False evidence.</i>

425
00:23:52,031 --> 00:23:54,364
I've arranged for one
of the best legal minds

426
00:23:54,433 --> 00:23:57,367
in the whole South
to defend you.

427
00:23:57,436 --> 00:23:58,335
Who?

428
00:23:58,404 --> 00:23:59,103
Me.

429
00:23:59,171 --> 00:24:00,171
You?

430
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:02,606
As I've often heard you say,

431
00:24:02,674 --> 00:24:04,575
you've got yourself
a mouthpiece.

432
00:24:04,643 --> 00:24:07,778
Julie... Now, don't
worry about a thing.

433
00:24:07,847 --> 00:24:09,379
You're in safe hands.

434
00:24:09,448 --> 00:24:12,416
Mr. Craven, is there a reason

435
00:24:12,484 --> 00:24:15,085
<i>why</i> nobody saw you
talking to Mr. Matlock?

436
00:24:15,154 --> 00:24:17,320
Yes, he told me...

437
00:24:17,389 --> 00:24:20,402
<i>I must say, Ben was a little
surprised... and he wasn't</i>

438
00:24:20,426 --> 00:24:22,437
<i>- the easiest client.</i>
- Order!

439
00:24:22,461 --> 00:24:24,172
It is, Your Honor!
It's a damn lie!

440
00:24:24,196 --> 00:24:25,796
Mr. Matlock!

441
00:24:25,865 --> 00:24:27,797
This jackass got
ahold of $50,000

442
00:24:27,867 --> 00:24:31,580
somewhere and somebody's
trying to railroad me in on it, and it's

443
00:24:31,604 --> 00:24:32,703
a damn lie!

444
00:24:32,772 --> 00:24:34,816
Mr. Matlock, you will
sit down immediately,

445
00:24:34,840 --> 00:24:36,606
or I'll cite you for contempt!

446
00:24:38,010 --> 00:24:40,044
<i>But we worked together.</i>

447
00:24:40,112 --> 00:24:42,123
I can't believe it,
losing control like that.

448
00:24:42,147 --> 00:24:44,226
If I was my lawyer, I'd fire me.

449
00:24:44,250 --> 00:24:45,416
I would, too.

450
00:24:45,484 --> 00:24:47,685
But I can't fire you.

451
00:24:47,753 --> 00:24:49,720
You're the only client I have.

452
00:25:17,950 --> 00:25:20,017
I think we got him.

453
00:25:21,487 --> 00:25:23,420
The chair.

454
00:25:23,489 --> 00:25:28,726
Remember... the executive chair?

455
00:25:28,794 --> 00:25:33,063
This chair will straighten
this whole thing out right now.

456
00:25:33,132 --> 00:25:34,876
You remember this chair? Mm-hmm.

457
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:37,167
Where Dan Keaton was sitting...

458
00:25:37,236 --> 00:25:39,003
when you shot him?

459
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:42,973
Mr. O'Hara...

460
00:25:44,643 --> 00:25:47,110
would you please
sit in the chair?

461
00:25:56,522 --> 00:25:58,422
There you go!

462
00:25:58,491 --> 00:25:59,890
Ah...

463
00:26:01,327 --> 00:26:02,826
Little low, isn't it?

464
00:26:04,163 --> 00:26:05,796
It's not my chair.

465
00:26:05,864 --> 00:26:07,063
That's right.

466
00:26:07,132 --> 00:26:08,966
This chair was set for somebody

467
00:26:09,034 --> 00:26:10,500
a lot taller.

468
00:26:10,569 --> 00:26:12,102
Thank you, Mr. O'Hara.

469
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:17,400
Mr. Linford, have a seat.

470
00:26:37,163 --> 00:26:39,363
Looks like a perfect fit.

471
00:26:39,432 --> 00:26:41,976
Has the jury reached a verdict?

472
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,499
We have, Your Honor.

473
00:26:43,569 --> 00:26:47,371
Will the defendant please stand?

474
00:26:50,409 --> 00:26:52,542
May we have the verdict?

475
00:26:52,612 --> 00:26:55,245
We find the defendant,
Benjamin Matlock,

476
00:26:55,314 --> 00:26:56,314
not guilty.

477
00:26:57,383 --> 00:26:59,160
Court is adjourned.

478
00:27:00,353 --> 00:27:03,620
So you see, Ben
Matlock is an honest man.

479
00:27:03,689 --> 00:27:06,156
You think Ben
Matlock is a great guy...

480
00:27:06,225 --> 00:27:08,424
and he's honest and he's loyal.

481
00:27:08,493 --> 00:27:10,426
Well, maybe he
is to his friends.

482
00:27:10,495 --> 00:27:12,173
But what about
the innocent people

483
00:27:12,197 --> 00:27:14,798
whose lives he's destroyed
with his brilliant legal mind?

484
00:27:17,369 --> 00:27:18,735
Answer it.

485
00:27:18,804 --> 00:27:21,049
If it's Matlock, tell him
there's an emergency

486
00:27:21,073 --> 00:27:23,251
and he has to
get here right now.

487
00:27:23,275 --> 00:27:24,708
Move!

488
00:27:30,015 --> 00:27:31,381
Hello?

489
00:27:31,450 --> 00:27:32,760
Hey there.

490
00:27:32,784 --> 00:27:35,519
You managing to
keep your powder dry?

491
00:27:35,588 --> 00:27:38,589
Ben, I'm glad you called.

492
00:27:38,658 --> 00:27:40,201
You must really
have a bad connection

493
00:27:40,225 --> 00:27:41,692
if you think I sound like Ben.

494
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:45,395
Listen, Ben, you have
to get over to the office

495
00:27:45,464 --> 00:27:47,931
right away; there's
a client waiting.

496
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:49,365
Understand, Ben?

497
00:27:49,435 --> 00:27:51,167
Something's wrong?

498
00:27:51,236 --> 00:27:52,903
You'll have to see for yourself.

499
00:27:52,972 --> 00:27:54,549
All right, you hang in there...
I'll be there in ten minutes.

500
00:27:54,573 --> 00:27:56,372
Thanks, Ben.

501
00:28:52,464 --> 00:28:54,130
Michelle?

502
00:29:22,561 --> 00:29:23,660
Hello, babe.

503
00:29:23,729 --> 00:29:25,629
Yeah, false alarm.

504
00:29:25,697 --> 00:29:28,632
Yeah, so warm it
up... I'm on my way in.

505
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:29,766
All right, bye-bye.

506
00:29:45,951 --> 00:29:47,917
Come on, let's go. Move it.

507
00:29:47,986 --> 00:29:49,586
Move it!

508
00:29:51,257 --> 00:29:53,189
Oh, Tyler.

509
00:29:54,460 --> 00:29:57,606
Here. Ooh... Are you all right?

510
00:29:57,630 --> 00:30:00,190
Yeah.

511
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:02,644
Well, it doesn't look too bad.

512
00:30:02,668 --> 00:30:04,579
Just give me a
minute; I'll be all right.

513
00:30:04,603 --> 00:30:05,869
He wants to kill Ben.

514
00:30:05,937 --> 00:30:08,183
Correction: I'm <i>gonna</i> kill Ben.

515
00:30:08,207 --> 00:30:09,840
Any particular reason?

516
00:30:09,908 --> 00:30:11,441
He didn't say.

517
00:30:11,510 --> 00:30:13,143
It's very peculiar.

518
00:30:13,212 --> 00:30:15,612
First he breaks into the office,

519
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:17,147
locks us in the closet,

520
00:30:17,216 --> 00:30:18,982
hits you over the head.

521
00:30:19,050 --> 00:30:22,363
Then he says he wants to kill
Ben, but he won't tell us why.

522
00:30:22,387 --> 00:30:24,499
Don't you think we
deserve an explanation?

523
00:30:24,523 --> 00:30:26,256
Do you always talk so much?

524
00:30:26,325 --> 00:30:28,258
Only when I'm nervous.

525
00:30:28,327 --> 00:30:30,660
Baby, relax... nothing's
gonna happen to you.

526
00:30:30,729 --> 00:30:34,164
I can't bear men I don't
know calling me "baby."

527
00:30:34,233 --> 00:30:35,810
Shut up. I'm sorry.

528
00:30:35,834 --> 00:30:37,834
Don't apologize;
just be quiet, okay?

529
00:30:37,870 --> 00:30:39,369
Whatever you say.

530
00:30:43,575 --> 00:30:44,974
Maybe<i> you</i> ought to relax.

531
00:30:45,044 --> 00:30:46,642
I am fine.

532
00:30:46,712 --> 00:30:48,632
You don't look so good.

533
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:50,880
You're sweating, you're on edge.

534
00:30:50,949 --> 00:30:53,061
Why are you doing
this to yourself?

535
00:30:53,085 --> 00:30:55,396
Why don't you think
about giving it up?

536
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:56,986
Forget it.

537
00:30:57,055 --> 00:30:58,855
You know, personally,

538
00:30:58,923 --> 00:31:01,491
I can understand someone
wanting to kill Ben Matlock.

539
00:31:01,559 --> 00:31:03,905
I've thought about it
once or twice myself.

540
00:31:03,929 --> 00:31:05,940
Tyler! No, really.

541
00:31:05,964 --> 00:31:09,099
You see, the world sees what
Ben Matlock<i> wants</i> it to see:

542
00:31:09,167 --> 00:31:12,702
the great defense
attorney, man for the people,

543
00:31:12,771 --> 00:31:15,738
willing to fight the
good fight for justice.

544
00:31:15,808 --> 00:31:18,708
Someone's got to do his legwork.

545
00:31:18,777 --> 00:31:20,688
Someone's got
to dig out the facts.

546
00:31:20,712 --> 00:31:22,779
Someone's got to do
the dirt around here.

547
00:31:22,848 --> 00:31:25,649
Ben, now, this is
exactly the reason

548
00:31:25,717 --> 00:31:27,884
why I don't like
investigative work.

549
00:31:27,952 --> 00:31:30,320
Take a look around here.

550
00:31:30,389 --> 00:31:32,489
One... this is the kind of place

551
00:31:32,557 --> 00:31:34,958
where you can catch
death like a cold!

552
00:31:35,026 --> 00:31:38,027
I assume there's a powerful
reason why we're here.

553
00:31:38,097 --> 00:31:39,363
Two...

554
00:31:39,431 --> 00:31:41,497
You never know when
one of these yahoos

555
00:31:41,566 --> 00:31:44,367
is gonna get it in his
skull to mess with you.

556
00:31:44,436 --> 00:31:47,037
Tyler, why are we here?

557
00:31:47,106 --> 00:31:50,173
All right... I put the
word out to some people

558
00:31:50,242 --> 00:31:53,977
that we are looking for
a very special songbird.

559
00:31:54,046 --> 00:31:56,023
I got a message on
my answering machine

560
00:31:56,047 --> 00:31:58,848
that said if we came
here we'd meet somebody.

561
00:31:58,917 --> 00:32:02,686
You think the person we're
looking for could be here now?

562
00:32:02,755 --> 00:32:04,688
Well, either that...

563
00:32:04,756 --> 00:32:07,957
or they sent someone
to scout us out first.

564
00:32:12,164 --> 00:32:13,931
You girls lost?

565
00:32:15,067 --> 00:32:16,700
You see what I mean?

566
00:32:16,769 --> 00:32:18,869
It never fails!

567
00:32:18,937 --> 00:32:21,004
I asked you a question,<i> Leroy.</i>

568
00:32:21,073 --> 00:32:22,605
Of course, you realize

569
00:32:22,674 --> 00:32:25,208
that because of this scholar,
we have blown our meeting.

570
00:32:25,277 --> 00:32:27,811
No one's gonna talk to us now.

571
00:32:31,116 --> 00:32:33,416
Let's you and me get it on, son.

572
00:32:33,485 --> 00:32:35,763
You know, friend, you're
right, you made your point.

573
00:32:35,787 --> 00:32:36,998
We're leaving. Excuse us.

574
00:32:37,022 --> 00:32:39,889
You ain't goin'...

575
00:32:42,728 --> 00:32:44,828
Damn, I hate this business.

576
00:32:47,799 --> 00:32:50,033
Now, I have been beat up,

577
00:32:50,102 --> 00:32:53,904
tied up and locked
up for good old Ben.

578
00:32:53,973 --> 00:32:55,750
<i>You see, he's great
at giving directions.</i>

579
00:32:55,774 --> 00:32:57,618
I'm still following her, Ben.

580
00:32:57,642 --> 00:32:58,808
Well, don't lose her.

581
00:32:58,877 --> 00:33:01,444
Hey, do you tell Julia
Child how to fry an egg?

582
00:33:01,513 --> 00:33:03,579
Is she with somebody?
Can you see?

583
00:33:03,649 --> 00:33:04,881
Not yet.

584
00:33:04,950 --> 00:33:05,882
Get closer.

585
00:33:05,951 --> 00:33:07,483
I'm trying.

586
00:33:07,553 --> 00:33:08,751
Can you see who it is?

587
00:33:08,820 --> 00:33:10,153
I'm working on it, Ben!

588
00:33:10,222 --> 00:33:12,233
Hey, I'm gonna take this
shortcut through the alley,

589
00:33:12,257 --> 00:33:13,723
try to get in front of her.

590
00:33:13,792 --> 00:33:15,057
Tyler?

591
00:33:16,328 --> 00:33:17,894
Tyler?

592
00:33:17,962 --> 00:33:19,796
Tyler?

593
00:33:21,566 --> 00:33:23,165
Tyler?

594
00:33:25,169 --> 00:33:26,202
Tyler?

595
00:33:27,306 --> 00:33:29,138
Tyler, are you all right?

596
00:33:29,207 --> 00:33:30,740
Yeah, yeah, I'm fine.

597
00:33:30,809 --> 00:33:32,575
But I think I lost 'em.

598
00:33:32,644 --> 00:33:34,043
Momentarily.

599
00:33:34,112 --> 00:33:36,947
And he's willing to go to
any length to find the truth.

600
00:33:37,015 --> 00:33:39,527
Ben, I was in the army
once... once is enough.

601
00:33:39,551 --> 00:33:41,329
What do you mean, I<i> have</i> to?

602
00:33:41,353 --> 00:33:42,486
You the new man?

603
00:33:42,554 --> 00:33:43,753
Looks that way.

604
00:33:43,822 --> 00:33:44,721
Name?

605
00:33:44,790 --> 00:33:46,790
Hudson, Tyler.

606
00:33:46,858 --> 00:33:47,691
Rank?

607
00:33:47,759 --> 00:33:49,892
Private.

608
00:33:49,961 --> 00:33:51,839
You get your butt into fatigues!

609
00:33:51,863 --> 00:33:54,964
I'm gonna make you my
special project, Private!

610
00:33:59,938 --> 00:34:01,048
Hear the news?

611
00:34:01,072 --> 00:34:02,305
I died?

612
00:34:02,374 --> 00:34:05,308
Five perfect
inspections in a row.

613
00:34:05,377 --> 00:34:07,255
Everybody's going
in town tonight.

614
00:34:07,279 --> 00:34:08,211
Not me.

615
00:34:08,280 --> 00:34:10,113
You're not? No way.

616
00:34:10,182 --> 00:34:12,949
The last 24 hours...

617
00:34:13,017 --> 00:34:15,452
I've cleaned two latrines,

618
00:34:15,520 --> 00:34:17,154
policed the parade grounds,

619
00:34:17,222 --> 00:34:20,290
run an obstacle course,<i> and...</i>

620
00:34:20,359 --> 00:34:22,692
painted the flagpole.

621
00:34:22,761 --> 00:34:24,361
My back is killing me.

622
00:34:24,429 --> 00:34:28,264
What used to be simple little
blisters on my hands and my feet

623
00:34:28,333 --> 00:34:29,933
are now bleeding sores.

624
00:34:30,001 --> 00:34:32,769
Now, as much as I
hate this little bunk,

625
00:34:32,838 --> 00:34:35,972
when I lay my
head on this pillow,

626
00:34:36,041 --> 00:34:40,009
it will not move until morning.

627
00:34:44,116 --> 00:34:45,156
Ten-hut!

628
00:34:46,284 --> 00:34:48,117
Gentlemen,

629
00:34:48,186 --> 00:34:50,286
there will be no night
on the town tonight.

630
00:34:50,355 --> 00:34:52,288
This platoon will
be in full field gear

631
00:34:52,357 --> 00:34:56,158
at 1800 hours, ready
for a little speed march.

632
00:34:56,227 --> 00:34:57,627
Carry on.

633
00:35:07,139 --> 00:35:10,273
I knew I'd find you
around here someplace.

634
00:35:10,342 --> 00:35:12,809
Don't talk to me.

635
00:35:12,878 --> 00:35:17,681
Tyler... I know how
unpleasant this is,

636
00:35:17,749 --> 00:35:21,484
and I can't tell you how much
I appreciate what you're doing.

637
00:35:21,553 --> 00:35:23,887
But somehow, I'll
make it up to you.

638
00:35:23,956 --> 00:35:27,757
I don't know how
yet, but I will, I swear.

639
00:35:30,395 --> 00:35:32,328
What are you looking at?

640
00:35:32,397 --> 00:35:33,796
You're gonna be mad.

641
00:35:33,866 --> 00:35:35,164
Try me.

642
00:35:35,234 --> 00:35:39,135
Well... you missed a spot.

643
00:35:40,672 --> 00:35:43,106
I told you... I knew
you'd be mad.

644
00:35:45,510 --> 00:35:48,478
<i>There's no one that's
too big or too tough</i>

645
00:35:48,546 --> 00:35:49,879
<i>for Ben Matlock.</i>

646
00:35:49,948 --> 00:35:52,248
You know, he told me
that you was his friend.

647
00:35:52,317 --> 00:35:55,352
I guess I better go
straighten him out about that.

648
00:36:14,706 --> 00:36:16,272
Let's talk.

649
00:36:16,341 --> 00:36:19,909
Mrs. Martinez been in today?

650
00:36:24,449 --> 00:36:27,183
Old Tyler Hudson is
prepared to buy him a drink.

651
00:36:31,857 --> 00:36:32,897
Tequila all around...

652
00:36:32,958 --> 00:36:35,458
courtesy of Tyler Hudson.

653
00:36:37,762 --> 00:36:39,406
Harold was going to fire
you, wasn't he, Jimbo?

654
00:36:39,430 --> 00:36:40,774
He said that you
are a hopeless drunk

655
00:36:40,798 --> 00:36:42,031
and he was going to make sure

656
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:43,820
that you never got
another job... Why you...

657
00:36:46,137 --> 00:36:47,270
Don't move, Jimbo.

658
00:36:47,339 --> 00:36:49,205
Don't move.

659
00:36:49,274 --> 00:36:50,307
See, I was in 'Nam.

660
00:36:50,375 --> 00:36:52,141
I'm the real thing.

661
00:36:52,210 --> 00:36:54,544
Green Beret, black
belt, Silver Star.

662
00:36:54,613 --> 00:36:57,079
I was a P.O.W. and an M.I.A.

663
00:36:57,148 --> 00:36:58,848
It was so bad where we were,

664
00:36:58,917 --> 00:37:01,817
we slept in body
bags, just in case.

665
00:37:01,886 --> 00:37:03,553
Don't you move, Jimbo.

666
00:37:03,622 --> 00:37:05,087
I'll take you out.

667
00:37:05,156 --> 00:37:09,993
But I want to know, just what
did Ben Matlock do to you?

668
00:37:10,062 --> 00:37:12,206
Ben, you in there?

669
00:37:12,230 --> 00:37:14,097
It's me, Les.

670
00:37:17,035 --> 00:37:18,379
Get rid of him.

671
00:37:18,403 --> 00:37:20,181
I got your mail.

672
00:37:20,205 --> 00:37:22,305
Don't try anything.

673
00:37:25,743 --> 00:37:26,754
Les.

674
00:37:26,778 --> 00:37:27,778
Hi.

675
00:37:27,845 --> 00:37:29,112
Wet enough for you?

676
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:31,080
I'm kind of busy, Les.

677
00:37:31,149 --> 00:37:32,982
Ben's not in yet?

678
00:37:33,051 --> 00:37:35,418
No. I'll tell him
you stopped by.

679
00:37:35,487 --> 00:37:39,855
Boy, this rain's got the traffic
all snarled up this morning.

680
00:37:39,924 --> 00:37:41,824
You've really got to go, Les.

681
00:37:41,893 --> 00:37:43,805
I know, you're...
you're really busy.

682
00:37:43,829 --> 00:37:45,061
But you go right ahead.

683
00:37:45,130 --> 00:37:46,629
Don't mind me.

684
00:37:46,698 --> 00:37:49,298
Just take me a couple
of minutes to fix this thing.

685
00:37:49,367 --> 00:37:52,268
This isn't a good time, Les.

686
00:37:52,337 --> 00:37:55,772
Aw, Ben's been complaining
about this for months.

687
00:37:55,840 --> 00:37:57,406
You can't do that now.

688
00:37:57,475 --> 00:37:59,209
I already am.

689
00:38:01,346 --> 00:38:05,047
Oh, this filter hasn't been
changed in a blue moon.

690
00:38:05,116 --> 00:38:08,384
You got a piece of
paper or something?

691
00:38:23,367 --> 00:38:24,501
Les?

692
00:38:27,405 --> 00:38:28,605
Oh, thanks.

693
00:38:31,209 --> 00:38:34,210
I'll bring in a new
filter tomorrow.

694
00:38:34,278 --> 00:38:36,880
Tell Ben that I said hello.

695
00:38:37,949 --> 00:38:39,315
Nice to see you.

696
00:39:00,605 --> 00:39:02,639
Come on, let's go.

697
00:39:14,252 --> 00:39:15,719
Sit down!

698
00:39:25,497 --> 00:39:26,563
Tie him up.

699
00:39:26,631 --> 00:39:27,831
Uh, I don't really know how...

700
00:39:27,899 --> 00:39:30,459
You're smart. Figure it out.

701
00:39:33,705 --> 00:39:35,238
What is taking Matlock so long?

702
00:39:35,307 --> 00:39:36,587
He's at the courthouse.

703
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:38,318
What's the matter, Ned,
you getting nervous?

704
00:39:38,342 --> 00:39:39,642
Not particularly.

705
00:39:39,711 --> 00:39:41,555
Maybe you're getting tired.

706
00:39:41,579 --> 00:39:43,524
You're thinking that
you're getting sloppy

707
00:39:43,548 --> 00:39:45,181
and might make a mistake.

708
00:39:45,249 --> 00:39:47,683
Why else would you tie me up?

709
00:39:49,654 --> 00:39:51,353
You know something else?

710
00:39:51,423 --> 00:39:52,722
I think you got a problem.

711
00:39:52,791 --> 00:39:53,956
Oh, really? What's that?

712
00:39:54,025 --> 00:39:55,736
I think you had
it all figured out.

713
00:39:55,760 --> 00:39:57,371
You knew what
you were going to do

714
00:39:57,395 --> 00:39:58,675
and why you were doing it,

715
00:39:58,730 --> 00:40:02,532
but now, you're not so sure.

716
00:40:02,600 --> 00:40:05,368
Maybe you want a way out.

717
00:40:05,437 --> 00:40:06,869
What makes you say that?

718
00:40:06,938 --> 00:40:09,083
'Cause you won't tell
us why you're here.

719
00:40:09,107 --> 00:40:10,472
You'll know when I'm ready.

720
00:40:10,541 --> 00:40:12,452
Well, what's the big
secret? Tell us. Shut up!

721
00:40:12,476 --> 00:40:13,709
Come on, what happened?

722
00:40:13,778 --> 00:40:14,855
What was this great injustice?

723
00:40:14,879 --> 00:40:16,412
What did Ben Matlock do to you?

724
00:40:16,481 --> 00:40:18,158
Shut up. Come on, I'm waiting.

725
00:40:18,182 --> 00:40:19,827
I'm going to gag
you. Then who, who

726
00:40:19,851 --> 00:40:21,417
did Ben Matlock hurt?

727
00:40:21,486 --> 00:40:23,526
My brother. Your brother?

728
00:40:24,455 --> 00:40:27,357
My brother hanged
himself in jail last week.

729
00:40:27,426 --> 00:40:30,092
Ned, Ben's a defense attorney.

730
00:40:30,161 --> 00:40:31,872
He doesn't put people in jail.

731
00:40:31,896 --> 00:40:34,096
He framed my brother.
That's impossible.

732
00:40:34,165 --> 00:40:36,525
Ned, would you tell us
who you're talking about?

733
00:40:38,703 --> 00:40:40,870
James Billings.

734
00:40:42,173 --> 00:40:44,106
The Erskine Tate case.

735
00:40:44,175 --> 00:40:45,741
Uh-huh.

736
00:40:50,448 --> 00:40:53,082
I'm going to tell
you what happened.

737
00:40:53,151 --> 00:40:54,918
My brother taught law.

738
00:40:54,986 --> 00:40:57,019
He was Professor
Tate's teaching assistant.

739
00:40:57,088 --> 00:41:00,489
He really cared for him.

740
00:41:00,559 --> 00:41:04,059
He even gave him a party
at the end of the semester.

741
00:41:04,128 --> 00:41:06,462
Here's to you, sir.

742
00:41:08,566 --> 00:41:10,011
<i>And that night at the party</i>

743
00:41:10,035 --> 00:41:12,535
<i>Professor Tate had a
little too much to drink,</i>

744
00:41:12,603 --> 00:41:14,015
<i>but he insisted on driving home.</i>

745
00:41:14,039 --> 00:41:16,805
<i>And on the way home, he
ran over and killed a man.</i>

746
00:41:40,031 --> 00:41:43,632
Professor Tate
was as guilty as sin.

747
00:41:43,701 --> 00:41:47,437
And the police had all the
evidence they needed to convict.

748
00:41:47,505 --> 00:41:49,672
But Tate had something
better than the facts,

749
00:41:49,741 --> 00:41:51,640
something better than the truth.

750
00:41:51,709 --> 00:41:54,476
He had Ben Matlock as
his friend and attorney.

751
00:41:56,080 --> 00:41:58,280
Ben. Ben Matlock.

752
00:41:58,349 --> 00:42:00,360
Professor Tate, what happened?

753
00:42:00,384 --> 00:42:02,484
What happened is they've
got me locked up in here

754
00:42:02,553 --> 00:42:04,419
like some common criminal.

755
00:42:04,488 --> 00:42:05,788
Well, Professor,

756
00:42:05,857 --> 00:42:07,300
if they think you've
committed a crime

757
00:42:07,324 --> 00:42:09,202
they'll lock you up whether
you're common or not.

758
00:42:09,226 --> 00:42:11,027
But I haven't
committed any crime.

759
00:42:11,095 --> 00:42:12,695
Not really.

760
00:42:12,764 --> 00:42:16,899
Professor, what
are you doing here?

761
00:42:16,968 --> 00:42:20,103
I don't really know. They...

762
00:42:20,171 --> 00:42:23,573
booked me for driving
under the influence of alcohol,

763
00:42:23,641 --> 00:42:25,975
minor property damage.

764
00:42:26,044 --> 00:42:28,510
Were you driving
under the influence?

765
00:42:28,579 --> 00:42:32,648
Well, I had a few
glasses of champagne.

766
00:42:32,717 --> 00:42:34,116
A few?

767
00:42:35,219 --> 00:42:36,919
You saw the whole thing?

768
00:42:36,988 --> 00:42:39,956
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I
was standing right here.

769
00:42:40,024 --> 00:42:43,192
I ran over to help him,
but he was dead already.

770
00:42:43,260 --> 00:42:46,006
<i>Matlock pulled out a
shabby bag of tricks.</i>

771
00:42:46,030 --> 00:42:48,564
<i>He made witnesses
doubt their own stories.</i>

772
00:42:48,633 --> 00:42:50,478
<i>He made other
witnesses afraid to talk.</i>

773
00:42:50,502 --> 00:42:52,735
Do I... do I know you?

774
00:42:54,572 --> 00:42:56,572
We... We've never met.

775
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:59,842
Could have sworn
I'd seen you before.

776
00:42:59,911 --> 00:43:01,244
But you're not sure?

777
00:43:01,312 --> 00:43:03,390
A lot of folks come in here.

778
00:43:03,414 --> 00:43:04,981
That's what I thought.

779
00:43:07,518 --> 00:43:09,396
Isn't it true that had
you not gone in there,

780
00:43:09,420 --> 00:43:11,031
the gas station attendant
would have been gone

781
00:43:11,055 --> 00:43:12,433
and would not have
seen the hit-and-run?

782
00:43:12,457 --> 00:43:13,756
I suppose so.

783
00:43:13,825 --> 00:43:15,303
Isn't it true that
you lied to this court

784
00:43:15,327 --> 00:43:17,192
because you're
part of a conspiracy

785
00:43:17,262 --> 00:43:19,022
to frame Professor Tate? No!

786
00:43:20,498 --> 00:43:25,834
Mr. Billings, isn't it
true that you knew

787
00:43:25,903 --> 00:43:29,906
that students Wallace
and Lane had plagiarized

788
00:43:29,975 --> 00:43:31,473
their paper? No.

789
00:43:31,542 --> 00:43:33,487
But you're something
of a legal scholar.

790
00:43:33,511 --> 00:43:35,077
Well, I still didn't spot it.

791
00:43:35,146 --> 00:43:37,313
Instead of throwing
them out of school,

792
00:43:37,381 --> 00:43:39,461
you forced them to
go along with your plan

793
00:43:39,517 --> 00:43:41,818
to drug Professor Tate
and frame him for murder.

794
00:43:43,822 --> 00:43:47,089
Mr. Matlock, you
know as well as I do

795
00:43:47,158 --> 00:43:50,426
that none of this
has any basis in fact.

796
00:43:50,495 --> 00:43:53,696
I know this...

797
00:43:53,765 --> 00:43:56,299
I know you killed
an innocent man

798
00:43:56,367 --> 00:43:57,527
to satisfy your warped need

799
00:43:57,569 --> 00:43:58,929
- for revenge.
- Objection.

800
00:43:58,970 --> 00:44:00,202
Sustained.

801
00:44:00,271 --> 00:44:03,773
And when he was finished,
he had railroaded my brother.

802
00:44:10,314 --> 00:44:14,617
I went to see James a week
before he took his own life.

803
00:44:14,686 --> 00:44:16,586
He told me he didn't do it.

804
00:44:16,654 --> 00:44:17,953
He was lying.

805
00:44:18,022 --> 00:44:19,222
How the hell would you know?

806
00:44:19,290 --> 00:44:21,168
Ned, may I go to
the desk? Sit down.

807
00:44:21,192 --> 00:44:22,703
Are you afraid
to hear the truth?

808
00:44:22,727 --> 00:44:24,060
I know the truth!

809
00:44:24,129 --> 00:44:26,796
Then you have nothing to fear.

810
00:44:38,843 --> 00:44:41,110
Here.

811
00:44:41,178 --> 00:44:43,879
This is a letter
from your brother.

812
00:44:43,948 --> 00:44:47,717
We received it about six
weeks after Erskine Tate's trial.

813
00:44:47,785 --> 00:44:50,119
Ned, your brother
blamed Erskine Tate

814
00:44:50,187 --> 00:44:52,554
for the death of your father.

815
00:44:52,624 --> 00:44:54,056
He hated him so much,

816
00:44:54,125 --> 00:44:55,891
he decided to
frame him for murder.

817
00:44:55,960 --> 00:44:59,361
<i>On the night of the party,
with the help of some students,</i>

818
00:44:59,431 --> 00:45:02,298
<i>James drugged the professor.</i>

819
00:45:02,366 --> 00:45:05,735
I think it is time for...

820
00:45:05,804 --> 00:45:07,403
We've actually done it.

821
00:45:07,472 --> 00:45:08,538
Shut up, Wallace.

822
00:45:08,606 --> 00:45:11,474
Easy, easy, the
party's just begun.

823
00:45:12,877 --> 00:45:14,397
It's okay. Let's go.

824
00:45:21,686 --> 00:45:24,753
<i>Then he put on
Tate's coat and hat.</i>

825
00:45:24,822 --> 00:45:27,033
<i>The students put Tate
in the backseat of his car</i>

826
00:45:27,057 --> 00:45:29,170
<i>while your brother got
in behind the wheel.</i>

827
00:45:29,194 --> 00:45:31,660
<i>They waited at a bus stop</i>

828
00:45:31,729 --> 00:45:33,762
<i>and then, posing
as Erskine Tate,</i>

829
00:45:33,831 --> 00:45:35,864
<i>your brother
intentionally ran over</i>

830
00:45:35,933 --> 00:45:37,766
<i>and killed an innocent man.</i>

831
00:45:55,786 --> 00:45:58,387
This is James' handwriting.

832
00:45:58,456 --> 00:46:01,524
Ned, give me the gun.

833
00:46:01,593 --> 00:46:05,427
I'm with the District
Attorney's office.

834
00:46:05,496 --> 00:46:07,441
I can make it easier for you.

835
00:46:08,499 --> 00:46:11,734
You're not a killer,
and you know it.

836
00:46:19,743 --> 00:46:22,010
You made the right decision.

837
00:46:27,452 --> 00:46:29,885
Oh, I'm sorry I'm late.

838
00:46:29,954 --> 00:46:33,422
My battery went dead, and
I had to call road service,

839
00:46:33,491 --> 00:46:37,560
and on a day like
today they take forever.

840
00:46:37,628 --> 00:46:39,061
Who are you?

841
00:46:39,130 --> 00:46:41,697
Ned Billings, Ben Matlock.

842
00:46:41,766 --> 00:46:44,533
Oh... No clients. Not now.

843
00:46:44,602 --> 00:46:46,402
I have to get back to court.

844
00:46:46,471 --> 00:46:48,938
I don't have time to
look at evidence now.

845
00:46:49,007 --> 00:46:51,474
Just tag it and bag it
and put it on the desk.

846
00:46:51,542 --> 00:46:53,809
Ben...

847
00:46:53,878 --> 00:46:56,178
What are you doing
tied to that chair?

848
00:46:56,247 --> 00:46:59,314
Ben... Tyler, I have to face

849
00:46:59,383 --> 00:47:01,350
a jury in less than an hour.

850
00:47:01,418 --> 00:47:03,853
I don't have time
to fool around.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
